How We're Using AI in the Development of Mech
=================================================

I've been working on Mech now for about 12 years. Not contiguously, but the first line of Mech source code was written in 2014 [CITATION NEEDED], so this has been a project that is very close to me. I've wrestled with it over the years, whether to keep working on it, or to let it go. You can see this in the history of the project, where there are stretches when I didn't work on it at all. But I always come back to it, because I feel like it's a project that has a lot of potential, and I want to see it through.

The most reccent period of idle time was around 2023 [CITATION NEEDED], when ChatGPT version 3.5 was released. While I was very interested in the technology, it opened questions about the future of the Mech language and programming languages in general. Would people need to write in programming languages anymore? Was there a need for them at all? What would become of Mech?

It took about a year for me to resolve those questions. I became resolved that yes, people will still need programming languages like Mech in the future; no, LLMs will not replace programming languages [LINK NEEDED]; and actually today after watching how reasoning agents work, I'd upgrade that to say that LLMs *cannot* replace programming languages because they use programming languages as reasoning tools [FOOTNOTE NEEDED].


How we worked on Mech before 2023
--------------------------------------


How we've used AI so far
--------------------------------------

started with some docs

Then autocomplete code with autopilot

used copilot to fill in large repetetive sections

then we designed the first bytecode prototype using chatGPT

implemented with chatgpt and copilot, but tested manually at every step 
ultimately created tech debt, was in the wrong place, was ultimately entirely replaced, but helped us prove the concept

that's a theme is that mech has been built incrementally with small tests proving concepts but we need to build the full system still and AI can help with that, like an auto enhance for code, you point it at a prototype and it fleshes it out, and by prototype I mean something that would still be larger than would fit in any one engineer's head or the AI concetxt window.

then we started using Codex in may to see how it could be used to build out the runtime. cloud to pro 5x to now 20x usage, so it's not cheap to do this work, and really I've found only the highest model does reasonable work. More on that later. So, it's expensive and that's scary having that kind of dependency. the new runtime represents most of that usage. It consists of about 200k LOC comprising code but most of that is actually tests and design documentation and also various scripts and development artifacts. 

So anyway, I know there are a lot of feelings about the use of AI in software projects, so I think the best thing possible is to just be transparent and forthright about how we're using it in Mech and what we can learn from the experience.



