What's going to change
--------------------------------------

1.

First, each post we write will have a section about authorship. We'll break it down into 3 categories: written by a human, edited by human minimal AI; written by human and AI, edited by both; and written by AI edited by AI. We'll also link specifically to the commit history so you can see how it was edited.

In general, docs will be AI generated because it's very easy to point the AI to the code and say "write a reference doc for this feature", and it often returns something reasonable. Allowing for them to be generated by AI means we can keep the docs up to date with the code, which has always been a huge problem for any software project.

The blog will mostly stay written by me, so it can serve as a way for me to communicate with the community, and the community can be sure they're hearing from a real person rather than from an AI. 

Although sometimes I do what I call pinball writing, or pinball programming where you write the beginning of a sentence and then let the AI fill in the rest as intended. This is after the game pinball, which is seemingly random to beginners but actually experts are able to control the ball and get it to do what they want. There was a point when pinball was actually banned because it was regarded as a gambling game, but it was later reclassified as a game of skill after an expert demonstrated that the ball could be controlled. So pinball writing is when you write the beginning of the sentence until the AI finally fills in the rest of the sentence as intended and then you press tab to fill it in. I think this is just a form of accelerated writing and I wouldn't attribute this to AI but just to say taht it was done that way.

